(2630659, 361037, 44579, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by DrAchoo:</b></div><hr/><i>Finally, remember that when you shoot your kids you will always see more in the picture than the average viewer because you are intimately connected.  Some subtle facial expression means a world more to you than to us. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This is not actually my kid (as I don''t have any kids). I have no idea who he actually is, or even if he is 10 years old. So there is no emotional connection.<br/> <br/>I guess DPC just doesn''t like bubbles. lol.</td>', 'Originally posted by DrAchoo:Finally, remember that when you shoot your kids you will always see more in the picture than the average viewer because you are intimately connected.  Some subtle facial expression means a world more to you than to us.   This is not actually my kid (as I don''t have any kids). I have no idea who he actually is, or even if he is 10 years old. So there is no emotional connection. I guess DPC just doesn''t like bubbles. lol.', '2006-07-17 17:36:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(2630667, 360437, 32754, '<td valign="top">you needed to experiment with levels and then with curves and brightness/contrast to get a little more contrast instead of the washed out looking grey...might have had more punch if you were to keep the color background: maybe just the green houses because of the complementary color of red...check on all my messed up images..haha.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-07-17 16:40:42.</i></td>', 'you needed to experiment with levels and then with curves and brightness/contrast to get a little more contrast instead of the washed out looking grey...might have had more punch if you were to keep the color background: maybe just the green houses because of the complementary color of red...check on all my messed up images..haha.', '2006-07-17 17:40:20.000000', '2006-07-17 17:40:42.000000', 0),
(2630713, 361304, 25754, '<td valign="top">This is so underrated...</td>', 'This is so underrated...', '2006-07-17 18:05:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(2630735, 360437, 22748, '<td valign="top">In response to <a href="//dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=428498" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">your request for help</a>, I reprocessed your image:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=365236" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/22748/thumb/365236.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/22748/thumb/365236.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/22748/thumb/365236.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>I suggest you save at a higher quality setting always (your JPEG is only ~30KB, but you can use up to 150KB, so make use of it).<br/> <br/>The editing steps: (in the free GIMP tool)<br/> <br/>- Open original.<br/>- Duplicate background layer. Unsharp mask with radius around 50, amount=1.4, threshold=0. This adds Local Contrasts Enhancement and can really reduce the muddy look on a lot of images.<br/>- Duplicate background layer. Add selective gaussian blur to reduce the JPEG artifact appearance.)<br/>- Duplicate background layer. Add gaussian blur at radius 5, change blending mode to soft light.<br/>- Create new layer, radial gradient fill followed by brushing with a soft round brush to create a grayscale vignette mask. Set blending mode to soft light.<br/> <br/>The layers'' opacities were adjusted throughout the process until I was happy with them.<br/> <br/>(Normally I would try the Curves tool first, but I wanted to do something different and ended up happy with the look never using the curves tool.)<br/> <br/>Hope this gives you some ideas. Have fun!! It''s a cool image BTW.</td>', 'In response to your request for help, I reprocessed your image:  I suggest you save at a higher quality setting always (your JPEG is only ~30KB, but you can use up to 150KB, so make use of it). The editing steps: (in the free GIMP tool) - Open original.- Duplicate background layer. Unsharp mask with radius around 50, amount=1.4, threshold=0. This adds Local Contrasts Enhancement and can really reduce the muddy look on a lot of images.- Duplicate background layer. Add selective gaussian blur to reduce the JPEG artifact appearance.)- Duplicate background layer. Add gaussian blur at radius 5, change blending mode to soft light.- Create new layer, radial gradient fill followed by brushing with a soft round brush to create a grayscale vignette mask. Set blending mode to soft light. The layers'' opacities were adjusted throughout the process until I was happy with them. (Normally I would try the Curves tool first, but I wanted to do something different and ended up happy with the look never using the curves tool.) Hope this gives you some ideas. Have fun!! It''s a cool image BTW.', '2006-07-17 18:16:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(2630836, 361226, 53316, '<td valign="top">I really liked the cuteness and the wide style (even though it is harder to see details, not many entries are formatted like this, and I liked the uniqueness).  One thing I might change is how the wheels on the bottom are not noticable on all - I don''t know if that involves better lighting or would need a crop on the bottom, but I would be interested to see how it looks with more consistancy across all the figures.  The title and the figure on the right work very nicely together and overall I liked this photo!</td>', 'I really liked the cuteness and the wide style (even though it is harder to see details, not many entries are formatted like this, and I liked the uniqueness).  One thing I might change is how the wheels on the bottom are not noticable on all - I don''t know if that involves better lighting or would need a crop on the bottom, but I would be interested to see how it looks with more consistancy across all the figures.  The title and the figure on the right work very nicely together and overall I liked this photo!', '2006-07-17 18:45:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2630845, 360946, 53316, '<td valign="top">I like your setup - it is interesting seeing how you did it, as it looks quite realistic, especially the "texture" on the bricks.  One thing that confused me was the smaller one sitting on top of the larger one - is that supposed to be a person and their child? In any case, I''m sure that''s just me and I didn''t vote down for that.  Also another thing that might be interesting (not better really, just interesting) to see is how it would look if the darker leg was towards the foreground (which, since it was already burnt, would involve flipping the whole thing around).</td>', 'I like your setup - it is interesting seeing how you did it, as it looks quite realistic, especially the "texture" on the bricks.  One thing that confused me was the smaller one sitting on top of the larger one - is that supposed to be a person and their child? In any case, I''m sure that''s just me and I didn''t vote down for that.  Also another thing that might be interesting (not better really, just interesting) to see is how it would look if the darker leg was towards the foreground (which, since it was already burnt, would involve flipping the whole thing around).', '2006-07-17 18:49:08.000000', NULL, 0),
